# wfg-log 1
# fixture network-cue — messages sent to somebody else's box, and what the show
# was told about them.
#
# The sixth replay fixture, and the first that is not about Go.dot's own state
# at all: every record below is about a lighting desk that is not here. Which is
# exactly why it is worth committing. A replay of this log re-applies every one
# of them against a mount table built from the bundle beside it, reaches the
# same tree, produces the same records — and PUTS NOTHING ON ANY WIRE.
#
# That last part is the point rather than a limitation. `wfg replay` is what
# somebody runs at three in the morning to find out why a cue misfired, on a
# laptop that may well still be plugged into the show network. A replay that
# also sent would move the rig while they were reading about it.
#
# GO on a network cue whose wait is `sent`. The run is created by the GO and
# reports done on the next tick, because that is when the flush that carried it
# has happened — the datagram left at the end of the tick that fired the cue.
A 40 0 udp:10.0.0.5:9000 go s:"H2K4M6NP"
A 41 1 engine run.ended s:"H2K4M6NP"
#
# GO on the next cue, which names a node the desk does not have. Somebody
# mistyped it, months ago, and nothing until now had any way of noticing.
#
# APPLIED AND THEN FAILED, which are two different things and both are true. The
# request was legal — a cue may name any address, and the desk's namespace is
# not Go.dot's to police at edit time — and the show could not honour it. A show
# with one wrong address in it is still a show somebody has to run tonight.
A 100 2 udp:10.0.0.5:9000 go s:"Q6RS8TW2"
A 101 3 engine run.failed s:"Q6RS8TW2" s:"bad-address"
#
# `cue.fire` on the third, which does not touch standby (§3.5: only GO moves
# it). The value is an integer and the node is an integer, so nothing is
# coerced; the fader above was a float written to a float.
A 200 4 cli cue.fire s:"M5TQ7XVA" s:"V3XY5Z7B"
A 201 5 engine run.ended s:"V3XY5Z7B"
#
# AND THE SAME NODE, WRITTEN BY A CLIENT RATHER THAN BY A CUE. One value-write
# command reaches the show's own nodes and somebody else's alike (§4.11), so
# this record is the same shape as any other node.set and is refused in the same
# words when it is wrong.
#
# The value is logged AS APPLIED: an integer 1 sent to a float node is `f:1`
# here, because that is what landed in the tree and what went out on the wire.
# A replay puts the identical bytes on the identical node.
A 250 6 udp:10.0.0.5:9000 node.set s:"/desk/fader" f:1
#
# A write the desk will not take. A meter is something a console tells you, not
# something you tell it, and mounted access defaults to read for exactly the
# reason this record exists: assuming a write is allowed is the assumption that
# breaks a show, and refusing a legal one costs a single message.
R 260 7 udp:10.0.0.5:9000 read-only node.set s:"/desk/meter" f:0.5
#
# And a node no mount holds, refused as an address rather than as a permission.
# The distinction is what tells somebody at half past seven whether to go and
# look at the cue or at the device.
R 270 8 cli bad-address node.set s:"/nowhere/at/all" f:0.5
